1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:02,440
What if a number could turn, not just

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:05,760
grow or shrink but spin? That's a complex

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,700
number, and in one minute you'll see it

4
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:10,960
isn't complex at all. Here's the puzzle. What

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:14,530
number times itself gives minus one? Square two,

6
00:00:14,530 --> 00:00:17,880
you get four. Square minus two, still four.

7
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:20,080
Nothing on the number line works. So we

8
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:23,250
give it a name, i. Watch what multiplying

9
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:25,910
by minus one does. It flips the line,

10
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:27,970
a half turn. So I should be half

11
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:30,810
of that, a quarter turn. Multiply one by

12
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:33,520
i, and it swings straight up again, and

13
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:36,000
it lands on minus one. Now the line

14
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,560
opens into a plane. Three plus two i

15
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:41,770
is three steps right, two steps up. Multiply

16
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:44,400
by i, and the arrow turns 90 degrees

17
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:46,830
to minus two plus three i. A complex

18
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:48,910
number is a point with a direction. i

19
00:00:48,910 --> 00:00:51,400
is a quarter turn, and two quarter turns

20
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:54,450
face backward. That's why I squared is minus

21
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:56,660
one, not imaginary, just rotation.

